we've covered plenty of graphic settings  here with game tech but today we're  dealing with how much of a game we can  actually see so allow me to expand your  horizons with a quick look into how  field-of-view effects our games and its  relationship with aspect ratio and we'll  touch on the increasingly popular ultra  wide screen this is the part where I  advise you to subscribe to GameSpot so  do yourself a favor if you're not  subscribed already feel the view or fov  is the angle at which you see the world  through the in-game camera measured in  increments of single degrees the higher  the fov the more peripheral vision you  get but it will make the center look  farther away increasing the fov too much  will make it seem as if you're looking  through a fisheye lens here's an example  from doom it has an fov slider ranging  from 90 to 130 here's what the game  looks like with four different fov  settings pay attention to the demon head  and the pillars on the left and right as  fov goes up you'll see more of the  pillars in your peripheral vision but  the demon head at the center appears to  be farther we would suggest testing out  different movs to find the right balance  oftentimes a setting in the middle of  the allowed range works well I often  find games to have a default  fov a little too low for comfort but not  every game will let you change it  through the menu you need to find a way  to increase fov if you're not given a  menu option either via a third-party  application or by editing a game's ini  file some research needs to be done  since every game is going to have a  different process but in many cases it's  simply not possible however older games  such as my all-time favorite deus ex  which uses the original Unreal Engine  looks pretty damn sweet when you edit  the text file to do widescreen with a  proper fov here is it running in 1080p  with an fov of 105 it's an advantage to  have a high fov in competitive shooters  since it literally displays more of the  surrounding area look back at old  footage of competitive quake and you'll  see most players using a fisheye type of  fov many modern competitive games like  Battlefield 1 titanfall 2 and overwatch  also incorporate an MOV slider  now we can't have a proper discussion of  fov without mentioning aspect ratio  because it essentially dictates the  proper fov for a game back in the day  games were mostly played with a  resolution in a four by three aspect  ratio it feels much more cramped today  since widescreen has been the standard  for quite some time we commonly used  resolutions like 1080p 1440p and 4k  which are sixteen by nine occasionally  playing a game in widescreen will feel  too zoomed in which means the widescreen  setting is essentially a cropped version  of four by three when this happens we  don't get the extra real estate 16 by 9  or 16 by 10 offers cranking up the fov  solves this problem as it lets us see  more screen space and spreads it across  the wider resolution this leads into the  increasing popularity of ultra wide 21  by nine monitors which covers even more  real estate than standard widescreen but  for 21 by 9 to make sense you'd have to  crank up the fov even more we then get  into a gray area where we start to ask  how much is too much  fov for example you can set overwatch to  a 21 by 9 resolution but it'll be a  cropped version of 16 by 9 since you  can't really set an extreme fov  therefore it prevents you from really  getting a competitive advantage with the  extra screen space but battlefield One's  21 by 9 support is flawless and properly  covers your peripheral vision ultra wide  resolutions will be more demanding on  your hardware because it will have to  project more pixels using a higher fov  without increasing resolution may  slightly affect performance since your  PC will have to render more of the  game's assets what resolution aspect  ratio and fov do you like to use in  games feel free to share your thoughts  in the comments give this video a thumbs  up and subscribe to our channel if you  haven't already and as always thanks for  watching
 

